# 060 · The documentation site

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Tester basics

## What

A documentation site for Thermite, built with [Astro Starlight](https://starlight.astro.build/) from `docs/_site/`.

- It is written for a person who has never seen Thermite.
- An agent can read all of it through `llms.txt` and `llms-full.txt`.
- It covers everything Thermite does at the commit where the work starts, and nothing that it does not do yet.

### Where things live

| Path | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/_site/package.json` and its lockfile | The site's own dependencies, pinned to exact versions. The core's dependencies and the images do not change. |
| `docs/_site/astro.config.mjs` | The site's configuration, sidebar and plugins |
| `docs/_site/src/content.config.ts` | The page schema. Every page must have a title, a description and a Diátaxis type, or the build fails. |
| `docs/_site/src/content/docs/` | Every page, in `tutorials/`, `how-to/`, `reference/`, `explanation/` and `project/`, plus the home page and Start here |
| `docs/_site/src/assets/` | Screenshots and images |
| `scripts/` | The docs checks |
| `.dev/site/` | The built site (git-ignored) |
| `docs/` | Repository docs: the docs index, how to write and check the docs, and the contributor material. The plans stay in `docs/plan/`. |

The site uses these plugins, or their equals if step 2 finds a better one:

- `starlight-llms-txt` for `llms.txt`, `llms-full.txt` and `llms-small.txt`;
- `starlight-links-validator`, which fails the build on a broken internal link or anchor;
- a Mermaid plugin, so diagrams render the same on GitHub and on the site.

Search is Starlight's built-in Pagefind. It needs no outside service.

The user pages now in `docs/` move into the site with `git mv`, so each page keeps one source and its history. GitHub still shows them as Markdown. These are the quick start, architecture, campaign modes, judging, kits, write a kit, the kit, contract and settings references, and the glossary. `docs/local-development.md` is split: its operator tasks become how-to guides in the site, and its contributor part stays in `docs/` for goal 008.

### The pages

Every page has one [Diátaxis](https://diataxis.fr/) type:

- **Tutorials** teach by doing.
- **How-to guides** help with one task.
- **Reference** describes the parts.
- **Explanation** says why.

The list below is where the work starts. Step 2 fixes it with the owner.

- **Hubs:** Home. Start here, which offers a path for each reader: try it on one computer, run it on your GPUs, give it your own project, or let an agent run Thermite.
- **Tutorials.** Each one is one path that works end to end:
  1. Your first run: start Thermite on your computer and watch the judge check a rig.
  2. Hand the work to agents, with a free model.
  3. Your own project, from the New campaign page.
  4. Your first goal campaign.
- **How-to guides:**
  - **Install and rigs:** Install Thermite on one machine (Docker or Podman). Add a rig on a machine over SSH. Add a rig on a Kubernetes cluster. Join a rig yourself where control cannot connect. Give a rig several GPUs. Build the images and push them to your own registry. Extend the harness image. Test a GPU on RunPod.
  - **Models:** Choose a model: a free model first, then a model server on your own machine, then API keys, then subscriptions. Connect a provider. Use a Claude subscription. Use a ChatGPT subscription. Give each agent its own model.
  - **Campaigns and kits:** Start a campaign from the dashboard. Start a campaign from the command line. Write a contract for each mode. Write a kit for your project. Test a kit with known candidates. Use a private repository. Set a budget or a stop time. Steer a run. Decide what waits for you: promotions, audits, reviews and "not possible" endings. Run a campaign on several rigs. Take the result.
  - **Operate:** Check a rig. Accept a rig's key. Set up the results repository. Configure advisors. Stop, restart, remove and fully reset a rig. Make and change tokens. Cancel a pending command. Troubleshoot: failed checks, port clashes, the Podman VM's disk and memory, slow builds, and where the logs are.
  - **Automate:** Let an agent run Thermite over MCP. Script Thermite with the REST API.
- **Reference:** Settings. The contract. The kit manifest (`kit.json`). The advisors file. The command line. The REST API. The MCP tools. Signed commands. Tokens and what each may do. Rig checks. Verdicts and attempt states. The harness image: its contents, toolchains and users. Ports, volumes and secret files. Limits and pacing. Compatibility: Node.js, container engines, compose tools and GPUs. The glossary.
- **Explanation:** What Thermite is. How the parts fit together: rigs, control and targets. Campaign modes. Why agents never judge. Why the benchmark lives in a kit, outside the project. Kits and rigs, side by side. Agents, lanes and the planner. Supervision: teachers, audits and review panels. Trust between control and rigs. Isolation inside a rig. Sharing a device. Why Thermite runs until the job is done. Who holds your keys. Where Thermite came from.
- **Project:** The roadmap and plans (links to `docs/plan/`). Getting help. Contributing, security and conduct (links to the files that goal 008 writes).

### Writing rules

- **One page, one type.** A short part of another type is fine. A page that has become two documents is split and linked. The page schema makes the type required. The maintainers' write-docs skill has a classify, write and audit procedure for this.
- **The opening says what the reader will do or learn, and why,** in the reader's words. What they need comes next.
- **Plain, everyday words and short sentences.** The first time a page uses a Thermite term, it explains the term or links it to the glossary. The aim is readable English, not strict Simplified Technical English.
- **Each step says what the reader sees after it.** Screens, buttons and fields are named exactly as the dashboard shows them.
- **Only what exists.**
  - Every command, flag, setting, route and field on a page exists in the code at the start commit.
  - A planned feature gets one line and a link to its goal. It is never described as if it existed.
- **No default caps, stated correctly.** No page calls a pacing or per-process limit something that ends research. Caps are off unless someone sets them. Pages that use a paid key say to set a budget or a stop time first.
- **Public content.**
  - Examples use made-up names: `gpu-host-1`, `rig-1`, `control.example.com`.
  - Screenshots come only from the dashboard demo's made-up data.
  - Known gaps go on goal 007's page, which the owner reviews. Other pages describe how things work.

### Work, in order

1. **Inventory** (read-only subagents, one for each area):
   - Areas: control and the dashboard; the REST API, MCP and the command line; the harness (judge, kits, contracts, agents); the supervisor (advisors, git, lifecycle); deployment (images, compose, machines, clusters, RunPod).
   - Each agent lists its area's features, commands, settings and screen labels, each with the file it comes from, and maps the existing docs onto the page list.
   - Notes go in `.dev/docs-work/`.
2. **Skeleton and mockup.**
   - Set up the Starlight project with pinned versions, the page schema and the plugins.
   - Write the sidebar with a stub for every page.
   - Write the home page, Start here, one finished page of each type, and the checks.
   - Show the owner the built site. **The owner approves the look and the page list before step 3.**
3. **Write** (subagents in parallel, up to 8 at a time):
   - One writer for each group of pages.
   - Each writer changes only its own files, and works from the inventory and the code.
4. **Verify** (fresh subagents):
   - For each page: check every claim against the code, run a Diátaxis audit, and read it for plain words.
   - Follow each tutorial, and each how-to guide that runs locally, end to end on a clean local stack. Use only local stand-ins, such as the acceptance tests' SSH container and kind cluster, never a real host or cluster.
   - A fresh reader that sees only the built site tries each tutorial and how-to guide, and reports where it got stuck.
   - Fix what they find and check again, until nothing is left.
5. **Integrate:**
   - Move the old pages and fix every link in the repository.
   - Update `docs/README.md`, `README.md`, `AGENTS.md` and `CONVENTIONS.md`. AGENTS.md says how to build and check the docs, and that a change in behaviour updates its page.
   - Add the docs build and checks to CI, and a workflow that deploys the site to GitHub Pages from `main`.

### Not in this goal

- **Pages for what is not built yet.** Each later goal adds or changes its own pages. For example: published images and the release bundle (010, 011), `thermite doctor` (013), rigs on RunPod (019–025), the Logs page (027).
- **The known-limits page and the security model:** goal 007 writes them into the site.
- **SECURITY.md, CONTRIBUTING.md and the other community files:** goal 008.
- **Reference generated from new code registries** (settings, events, OpenAPI): goals 029 and 050.
- **Other things left out:** versioned docs, a custom domain, translations and video.
- **Changes to Thermite itself.** A gap found while writing becomes a new goal.

## Why

The docs today are twelve dense pages, written for the people who built Thermite. A tester who has never seen it needs a way in: what it is, a first run that works, then how to do each task. Agents that install, operate or work on Thermite need the same facts, in one file they can read whole.

## Done when

- [ ] The owner approved the look and the page list. The approval is in Decisions.
- [ ] `npm run docs:build` builds the site from `docs/_site/` with pinned versions, with no errors or warnings. The page schema and the link validator pass.
- [ ] Every page on the approved list exists. Each has a title, a one-line description and one type, and sits in its type's section.
- [ ] The inventory maps every feature built at the start commit to at least one page.
- [ ] `npm run docs:check` passes. It checks:
  - that each page sits in the section of its type;
  - that `llms-full.txt` holds every page;
  - that every `thermite` command on a page exists;
  - that every setting a page names exists in the code, and that every setting the code reads is in the settings reference;
  - that every contract, `kit.json` and `advisors.json` example parses with the real schema.
- [ ] The built site has `/llms.txt` in the [llms.txt format](https://llmstxt.org/), which leads an agent to every page, and `/llms-full.txt` with every page in full. Each page also has a Markdown copy at its address plus `.md`.
- [ ] Search works in the built site with no outside service.
- [ ] Each tutorial was followed end to end on a clean local stack, and each how-to guide that runs locally was followed once. Progress records what ran, and which pages need paid services or real hardware and were not run.
- [ ] A fresh reader that saw only the built site found no step that it could not follow, or each of its findings is fixed.
- [ ] The old user pages are moved, and no tracked file links to a moved page. `docs/README.md`, `README.md`, `AGENTS.md` and `CONVENTIONS.md` point to the site.
- [ ] CI builds and checks the docs. A workflow deploys the site to GitHub Pages from `main`.
- [ ] `npm run check` passes, and the public check passes over the new files.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-19, owner: One documentation site, built from `docs/_site/`. Repository docs stay in `docs/`, and the plans in `docs/plan/`.
- 2026-10-19, owner: The site is built with Astro Starlight, not MkDocs.
  - The owner first asked for MkDocs. Then the state of MkDocs was reviewed:
    - MkDocs no longer makes releases that work with its plugins;
    - Material for MkDocs gets security fixes only until 2027-05-05;
    - Zensical, the successor, is still young.
  - Starlight runs on Node and TypeScript, like the rest of Thermite. Its page schema can require a Diátaxis type, and a maintained plugin writes `llms.txt`.
- 2026-10-19, owner: The pages follow Diátaxis: tutorials, how-to guides, reference and explanation.
- 2026-10-19, owner: The docs must be approachable for an average person. Agents read them through `llms.txt` and `llms-full.txt`.
- 2026-10-19, owner: Subagents write the pages in parallel. This goal is written first and run with `/goal`.

## Open questions

- **Starlight upgrades.** Starlight is still 0.x, so a minor version can break things. Recommended: pin exact versions, and upgrade on purpose, one version at a time.
- **The address.** Recommended: the repository's GitHub Pages address until a domain is chosen. Turning Pages on needs the owner's GitHub account.
- **The old pages.** Recommended: move them with `git mv`, so each page has one source. Copies left in `docs/` would drift.
- **Merged goals.** This goal takes in goal 006 (Docs for testers) and goal 041 (Kits and rigs, explained): their pages are on the list above, and the owner's choices in 006 carry over. Both are marked `DROPPED` as merged. Goal 050 keeps only the two-command start and a generated settings reference.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written. It draws on the owner's request, the earlier private publishing plan's documentation section, and the current docs.
- 2026-10-19: Work started from `7a2bc35`. Step 1 (inventory) and the Starlight skeleton run first.
- 2026-10-19: Step 1 done. Five read-only agents inventoried control and the dashboard, the API, MCP and command line, the harness, the supervisor, and deployment and settings, with file and line evidence. Their notes are in the maintainers' working folder.
- 2026-10-19: Step 2 built, waiting for the owner's review:
  - the Starlight project with pinned versions (Astro 7.3.8, Starlight 0.42.6), the page schema, the link validator, `llms.txt` with every page, `llms-full.txt`, `llms-small.txt`, one file per section and a Markdown copy of each page;
  - all 73 pages in the sidebar, as stubs where not yet written; the ten old user pages moved in with `git mv`;
  - the home page, Start here and one finished page of each type: Your first run, Check a rig, Tokens and roles, Why agents never judge;
  - `npm run docs:check`, a CI job and a GitHub Pages workflow;
  - `docs/writing-docs.md`, the guide every writer follows.
- 2026-10-19: Tutorial 1's steps ran from a fresh clone on a local Podman stack: the isolation check passed at start, and the smoke check passed with all 12 candidates judged as expected in about 100 seconds on a laptop CPU.

